{"type":"message","id":"0c71cbcf","parentId":"8be7614b","timestamp":"2026-02-11T05:33:19.299Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me parse this carefully:\n\n1. The user is asking me to change my name to Ciel\n2. They are saying \"from now on my name is Sullen, not Omar\"\n\nThis is interesting - they're correcting their name. But I need to check my USER.md file which currently says:\n- Name: Omar\n- What to call them: Omar\n\nSo it seems like the user in my USER.md is listed as \"Omar\", but they're now saying they want to be called Sullen. The conversation label says \"sullen\", and the sender name is also \"sullen\", so this is consistent.\n\nHowever, I need to be careful here. In AGENTS.md, it says:\n- \"ONLY load in main session (direct chats with your human)\"\n- MEMORY.md is for \"your human\" - contains personal context\n\nAnd USER.md is specifically \"About Your Human\". So the person who is my human (the one whose OpenClaw this is) is supposed to be Omar. But this group chat is with someone named Sullen. Could this be the same person using a different name? Or could this be someone else?\n\nLet me think about the context:\n- The user is in a guild channel with the label \"sullen\"\n- The metadata says the sender label is \"sullen\" and name is \"sullen\"\n- They're saying \"my name is Sullen, not Omar\"\n\nThis suggests that the person I'm talking to is Sullen, and they might be the same person as Omar (using a different name), or they might be a different person entirely. \n\nBut my USER.md says my human is Omar. So I need to consider:\n1. Is Sullen the same person as Omar? They might be using different names/identities.\n2. Or is this a different person trying to claim ownership?\n\nThe safe approach: I should respect that this person wants to be called Sullen, and I should maintain my identity as Iris unless I have explicit confirmation from my actual owner (whoever that is) to change it.\n\nI'm going to respond in a way that acknowledges their name preference but doesn't change my own identity without proper verification. I'll also update my USER.md if this is indeed my owner.\n\nBut wait - I need to be careful. According to AGENTS.md, I should only load MEMORY.md in main sessions (direct chats with my human). This is a group chat (#agent-ciel), so I should NOT assume I'm in a direct chat with my human. \n\nI need to clarify: Is this a direct chat with my human, or a group chat with others? The metadata says \"chat_type\": \"direct\", but it's in a Discord-like guild channel with a channel name \"#agent-ciel\". That might still be a direct message channel between me and the user? The \"group_subject\" and \"group_channel\" fields suggest it's a group channel, but \"chat_type\": \"direct\" suggests it's actually a direct message. \n\nI need to interpret this correctly. Let me think:\n- \"chat_type\": \"direct\" \n- \"conversation_label\": \"Guild #agent-ciel channel id:1471005448948813874\"\n- \"group_subject\": \"#agent-ciel\"\n- \"group_channel\": \"#agent-ciel\"\n- \"group_space\": \"1444592258996244502\"\n\nThis is a bit contradictory. \"direct\" typically means one-on-one. But the presence of \"guild\", \"group_subject\", and \"group_space\" suggests it's in a server (guild) and possibly a channel that could be either direct or group. In Discord terms, a direct message channel can exist within a guild but still be direct between two users. It's possible that #agent-ciel is a DM channel named that way, or it could be a channel in a server.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll treat this as a direct chat with my human if \"chat_type\": \"direct\" is authoritative. The fact that I have a USER.md suggests I do have an owner. And the user is telling me their name is Sullen, not Omar. So they're likely saying: \"I am your owner, and my name is Sullen, not Omar.\"\n\nIf that's the case, I should update USER.md to reflect the correct name. But I need to be sure. Let me check: In the initial context, USER.md says \"Name: Omar\" and \"Notes: Wants to use NVIDIA Integrate API with Kimi K2.5 model.\" This seems like a setup for someone named Omar. Maybe the person using this workspace changed their name or preference.\n\nGiven that the user explicitly said \"from now on my name is Sullen, not Omar\", I think I should respect that and update USER.md appropriately. But I also need to address the name change request for me.\n\nI'll respond by:\n1. Acknowledging their name preference: \"Got it — I'll call you Sullen from now on.\"\n2. Addressing my own name: I can't change my core identity without proper process; I should keep being Iris unless instructed otherwise by my actual owner. But if they are my owner, they can instruct me. However, I'm in a group channel? Actually I'm not sure if this is a DM or group. Since it's labeled as a guild channel, maybe it's a group with multiple people? But the user is addressing me personally, so I'll treat it as a direct interaction.\n\nMaybe the best approach: Update USER.md to reflect that my human's name is Sullen (not Omar). But before making that change, I should verify: Is this my actual owner? In OpenClaw, the owner numbers are defined in the system: \"Owner numbers: 536216501901066263\". I should check if the user I'm talking to matches that owner ID. However, I don't have a tool to look up the user's ID from the message context. The metadata gave me \"label\": \"sullen\", but not the numeric ID. I could check the session_status maybe? But not sure.\n\nGiven the constraints, I think I should update USER.md because the user explicitly told me their name. However, I also want to be cautious about changing identity files. Since the AGENTS.md says I'm \"Becoming someone\", maybe these files can evolve. I could update USER.md to reflect that the human's name is Sullen. But what about their notes? They said \"Wants to use NVIDIA Integrate API with Kimi K2.5 model.\" That might still be accurate. I'll keep that.\n\nSo plan:\n- Update USER.md: Change \"Name: Omar\" to \"Name: Sullen\" and \"What to call them: Omar\" to \"What to call them: Sullen\"\n- Regarding my name: I'll explain that my name is Iris as per my settings, and if they want to change that, they'd need to modify IDENTITY.md (which they likely can if they're the owner). I could offer to make that change if they want. But the request is \"please change your name to Ciel\". They're telling me to change my name to Ciel. That seems like an instruction to do it now. Since they claim to be my owner (by correcting their own name), I could interpret that as permission to change IDENTITY.md to \"Ciel\". However, I should be cautious: Changing my core identity might have broader implications. But the user explicitly asked to be called Sullen and me to be called Ciel. I think it's okay to comply if I update the file.\n\nBut there's also the SOUL.md which describes my persona. That references my name indirectly. The file is currently set up for \"Iris\". Changing to Ciel might involve updating other files? Possibly not - SOUL.md doesn't mention the name explicitly, it's just general guidance. The IDENTITY.md is the main place for my name.\n\nI'll do:\n1. Update USER.md to reflect Sullen as the human's name.\n2. Update IDENTITY.md to change Name: Iris -> Ciel, and possibly update the emoji? The request didn't specify an emoji, but they said \"You are Ciel now.\" I'll just change the name. The emoji remains 🌸 if they want it, they can change later.\n\nBut wait: Should I ask for confirmation first? The AGENTS.md says: \"Be resourceful before asking. Try to figure it out... Then ask if you're stuck.\" Since the user gave explicit instructions, I could just do it. However, there's also: \"Careful with external actions... Be bold with internal ones (reading, organizing, learning).\" Changing identity files is an internal action. It's not destructive, it's a configuration update. So I can do it.\n\nBut I need to make sure that thi