\nBut I should also be careful: If there are many messages, I should pace myself to not hit rate limits. I'll add a small delay using exec sleep? Actually I can just call sequentially with a short wait between. But since I'm in a single turn, I can't use `exec` to sleep easily. I could do a loop with `process` but that's overkill. Simpler: Do one batch, report, then continue in subsequent turns? But I want to complete in this turn if possible. The user expects me to delete all now.\n\nBut there might be a limit: the gateway tool call might time out if too many operations. However, I'm executing tool calls in sequence within one assistant turn. That's okay, but I need to be aware of total execution time. I'll start with a moderate batch size, say 50, and then continue until done.\n\nI'll implement a simple loop: fetch up to 100, delete each, then fetch more until done.\n\nLet's start with search: `channel: 1471005448948813874`, `limit: 100`. Also I might want to include `archived: false`? Probably not needed.\n\nAlso, `message.search` may require `fromMe: false` to get all messages? The default is all.\n\nI'll do:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"search\",\n  \"channel\": \"1471005448948813874\",\n  \"limit\": 100\n}\n```\n\nBut note: channel in Discord is a snowflake string. The tool expects string channel ID.\n\nLet's call.\n</think>\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e4d695db7f804e76a9cc26f4","name":"message","arguments":{"action":"search","channel":"1471005448948813874","limit":100}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"stepfun-ai/step-3.5-flash","usage":{"input":99240,"output":822,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":100062,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1770788412222}}
