{"type":"message","id":"b02ab4bf","parentId":"5064484a","timestamp":"2026-02-11T05:43:40.826Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":" not installed. I can still parse with grep and cut? But it's easier to use a small script that just loops and uses curl to fetch and delete without needing to parse JSON for IDs? I need to extract message IDs from JSON. Without jq, I can use `grep` and `sed` or `awk` to extract `\"id\"` fields. That's doable but messy. But maybe I can do it in multiple steps: first fetch and store IDs in a file, then read file and delete. That could be done.\n\nBut I need to run a series of commands. Since I have exec, I can run a single shell command string that includes the whole loop in bash. But the `exec` tool runs a single command; I can provide a multi-line script if I use `-c` with bash. I can do:\n\n`bash -c '...script...'`\n\nThe environment likely has bash.\n\nLet's construct a script:\n\n- channel_id=1471005448948813874\n- token=... (from config)\n- base_url=\"https://discord.com/api/v10\"\n\nFetch messages:\n- set before=\"\"\n- while true:\n  - if [ -z \"$before\" ]; then url=\"$base_url/channels/$channel_id/messages?limit=100\"; else url=\"$base_url/channels/$channel_id/messages?limit=100&before=$before\"; fi\n  - response=$(curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bot $token\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \"$url\")\n  - Check if error (e.g., empty, or contains \"code\")\n  - Extract IDs: maybe use grep -o '\"id\":\"[^\"]*\"' | cut -d'\"' -f4 > ids.txt\n  - Count number of IDs; if 0 break.\n  - For each id in ids.txt: curl -X DELETE -s -H \"Authorization: Bot $token\" \"$base_url/channels/$channel_id/messages/$id\"\n  - Set before to the last ID from this batch (the smallest ID, i.e., the oldest) -> we can take the last line from ids.txt.\n\nBut careful: Discord's order is descending (newest first). So the first batch: newest first. The last ID in the list is the oldest. Use that for before.\n\nNow about rate limits: we may need to handle 429 responses. Could add a simple retry with sleep if we get 429. But maybe not needed for a small channel. Could also use bulk delete if all messages <14 days. But we don't know. Let's do individual deletes.\n\nThe script will be somewhat long but okay.\n\nBut before that, we must ensure that the bot token we have is actually a bot token and not a user token that might have limited scopes. The token in config is under `accounts.default.token`. That likely is the bot token. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Discord API requires the bot to have the `MANAGE_MESSAGES` permission. We enabled `actions.moderation`, but that's for OpenClaw tool policy. The bot's underlying Discord permissions are set in Discord server settings. Did we check that? The user hasn't confirmed the bot has the Manage Messages permission in Discord. I earlier said I need that confirmation. The user didn't provide; they just said \"yes, delete all\". I assumed they'd confirm that the bot has the permission. But we never verified. The bot might not have that permission. Let's check: In the channel's permission overwrites, we see a member overwrite for the bot ID allowing VIEW_CHANNEL, but we need MANAGE_MESSAGES. The bot's role may have that permission at the guild level. But the channel's overwrite only denies VIEW_CHANNEL for @everyone, but other permissions are inherited from the bot's role. If the bot's role has MANAGE_MESSAGES, it should be able to delete. However, if the channel has permission overwrites that deny MANAGE_MESSAGES for the bot's role or @everyone, then it might be denied. The overwrites shown only affect VIEW_CHANNEL (1024). They don't mention MANAGE_MESSAGES (2048? Actually MANAGE_MESSAGES is 8192? Let's check: Discord permission bits: VIEW_CHANNEL=1024, MANAGE_MESSAGES=8192? Actually bitwise: ADMINISTRATOR=8, VIEW_CHANNEL=1024, SEND_MESSAGES=2048, MANAGE_MESSAGES=8192? Not sure. Anyway, the overwrites shown have deny for VIEW_CHANNEL only. So other permissions like MANAGE_MESSAGES would be inherited from the bot's role. So if the bot's role has MANAGE_MESSAGES at the guild level, it should be able to delete in this channel because the channel overwrite does not deny it. So likely it's okay. But we haven't confirmed.\n\nNevertheless, the user gave go-ahead. We'll attempt; if fails, we'll report errors.\n\nNow, we need to write a script. Let's craft a bash one-liner or multi-line. Use exec with a script that reads token from config file? Could read token from the config file we already have. But easier: I can embed the token we already extracted. Let's embed it.\n\nI'll create a script: \n\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\nCHANNEL_ID=\"1471005448948813874\"\nTOKEN=\"MTQ3MDk5MDc0MzA5ODIzMzExMg.GS1nWd.02_4znxHWOy_xbn5yVqx9UScxQQNLyrsyr80Ys\"\nBASE=\"https://discord.com/api/v10\"\n\n# Get messages, delete in batches\nBEFORE=\"\"\nTOTAL_DELETED=0\n\nwhile :; do\n  if [ -z \"$BEFORE\" ]; then\n    RESPONSE=$(curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bot $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \"$BASE/channels/$CHANNEL_ID/messages?limit=100\")\n  else\n    RESPONSE=$(curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bot $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \"$BASE/channels/$CHANNEL_ID/messages?limit=100&before=$BEFORE\")\n  fi\n\n  # Check if response is valid JSON and has an array of messages\n  # Extract IDs\n  IDs=$(echo \"$RESPONSE\" | grep -o '\"id\":\"[^\"]*\"' | cut -d'\"' -f4)\n  COUNT=$(echo \"$IDs\" | wc -l)\n\n  if [ \"$COUNT\" -eq 0 ]; then\n    break\n  fi\n\n  # Delete each message\n  for ID in $IDs; do\n    curl -s -X DELETE -H \"Authorization: Bot $TOKEN\" \"$BASE/channels/$CHANNEL_ID/messages/$ID\" >/dev/null\n    # Rate limit: small delay\n    sleep 0.2\n  done\n\n  TOTAL_DELETED=$((TOTAL_DELETED + COUNT))\n  # Set BEFORE to the last ID (the oldest in this batch)\n  BEFORE=$(echo \"$IDs\" | tail -n1)\n\n  echo \"Deleted $COUNT messages (total: $TOTAL_DELETED)...\"\ndone\n\necho \"Finished. Total messages deleted: $TOTAL_DELETED\"\n```\n\nBut I need to ensure the token prefixed with \"Bot \" is correct for bot tokens. Usually it's \"Bot <token>\". That's correct.\n\nNow, run this via exec. But I must be careful: The token includes a dot, that's fine.\n\nLet's execute it.\n</think>\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00fb437376ff415c9101e815","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"bash -c '\nCHANNEL_ID=\"1471005448948813874\"\nTOKEN=\"MTQ3MDk5MDc0MzA5ODIzMzExMg.GS1nWd.02_4znxHWOy_xbn5yVqx9UScxQQNLyrsyr80Ys\"\nBASE=\"https://discord.com/api/v10\"\nBEFORE=\"\"\nTOTAL_DELETED=0\nwhile :; do\n  if [ -z \"$BEFORE\" ]; then\n    RESPONSE=$(curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bot $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \"$BASE/channels/$CHANNEL_ID/messages?limit=100\")\n  else\n    RESPONSE=$(curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bot $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \"$BASE/channels/$CHANNEL_ID/messages?limit=100&before=$BEFORE\")\n  fi\n  IDs=$(echo \"$RESPONSE\" | grep -o \"\\\"id\\\":\\\"[^\\\"]*\\\"\" | cut -d'\"' -f4)\n  COUNT=$(echo \"$IDs\" | wc -l)\n  if [ \"$COUNT\" -eq 0 ]; then\n    break\n  fi\n  for ID in $IDs; do\n    curl -s -X DELETE -H \"Authorization: Bot $TOKEN\" \"$BASE/channels/$CHANNEL_ID/messages/$ID\" >/dev/null\n    sleep 0.2\n  done\n  TOTAL_DELETED=$((TOTAL_DELETED + COUNT))\n  BEFORE=$(echo \"$IDs\" | tail -n1)\n  echo \"Deleted $COUNT messages (total: $TOTAL_DELETED)...\"\ndone\necho \"Finished. Total messages deleted: $TOTAL_DELETED\"\n'","timeout":300}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"stepfun-ai/step-3.5-flash","usage":{"input":118330,"output":2063,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":120393,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1770788589774}}
