{"type":"message","id":"09ef4a38","parentId":"0000b18e","timestamp":"2026-02-11T05:48:00.067Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":" curl output shows the channel still has messages, including my recent ones. The script claimed it deleted 117 messages, but it appears it either didn't actually delete anything or the channel still has messages. The output shows some messages, but not necessarily all.\n\nThe fact that the script output said \"Deleted 117 messages\" suggests the script ran and thought it deleted them. But the channel still has messages. Could be that the script deleted messages but then more messages appeared (new ones). Or perhaps the script didn't have permission, and the curl delete calls returned errors but the script didn't check them.\n\nLet's examine the script: it used curl to DELETE each message ID. It didn't check for HTTP errors; it just ran the curl and ignored output. So even if the deletion failed (due to permissions or rate limiting), the script would still count it as deleted. That's a flaw.\n\nWe need to fix the script to:\n1. Check HTTP response codes\n2. Handle rate limiting (HTTP 429) by reading Retry-After header and waiting\n3. Verify that each delete succeeded (204 No Content is success)\n4. Possibly use exponential backoff\n\nBut before we do that, I need to check if any of those 117 messages were actually deleted. Perhaps some were deleted but then the user can't see because of caching? Unlikely.\n\nBetter approach: Use the Discord API directly to bulk delete? There's a bulk-delete endpoint but it only works for messages younger than 2 weeks and up to 100 at a time. That would be more efficient and less rate-limit prone.\n\nGiven the user's concern, I should first verify the current message count. Let's fetch all message IDs and count them.\n</think>\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ae94910c74bd49febee40cfd","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bot MTQ3MDk5MDc0MzA5ODIzMzEx