{"type":"message","id":"32a90dea","parentId":"745f242c","timestamp":"2026-02-11T05:51:02.729Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":". The script fetched 202 messages but some deletes are failing with 404. This is odd because 404 typically means the message doesn't exist (maybe it was already deleted, or the ID is invalid). Could be that the message IDs are not being parsed correctly? Let me check the grep extraction.\n\nLooking at the script: `ids=($(echo \"$response\" | grep -o '\"id\":\"[^\"]*\"' | cut -d'\"' -f4))` This should extract all IDs. But maybe there are duplicate IDs? The output shows \"Failed to delete 536216501901066263\" which is a user ID, not a message ID. That suggests my parsing is picking up the wrong field. The message ID is in the \"id\" field at the top level of each message object. But the JSON might contain other \"id\" fields like in author, or other nested objects.\n\nI need to be more precise. Let's fetch a small sample and inspect the JSON structure. The earlier curl output showed messages like:\n`{\"type\":0,\"content\":\"...\",\"id\":\"1471019799114154045\", ... \"author\":{\"id\":\"536216501901066263\",...}}`\n\nSo there are two \"id\" fields: one for the message itself, and one for the author. My grep picks up all, so the array contains both message IDs and author IDs. That's why we see 404 on author IDs (they're not message IDs). I need to extract only the top-level id field.\n\nBetter approach: Use a proper JSON parser like `jq`. But jq is not installed. I can use Python? Probably not either. I could use a regex that ensures the id is at the start of an object field after {, but the JSON is an array of objects. A simpler fix: after getting all ids, deduplicate? Not reliable.\n\nActually, the message objects are like:\n```\n{\n  \"id\": \"1471019799114154045\",\n  \"channel_id\": \"...\",\n  ...\n  \"author\": {\n    \"id\": \"536216501901066263\",\n    ...\n  }\n}\n```\nSo the first occurrence of \"id\" in each object is the message id. But my grep finds all, order may interleave. Let's count: If we got 202 messages, we should have ~202 message IDs, but also ~202 author IDs. So total ~404 matches. The script might be trying to delete both.\n\nI need to extract only the message IDs. Since we're in bash without jq, I could use a more careful grep: match `\"id\":\"` but not when preceded by `\"author\":`? That's tricky.\n\nAlternative: Use a different approach: when we fetch messages, we can pipe through `sed -n 's/^.*\"id\":\"\\([^\"]*\\)\".*$/\\1/p'` but still same issue.\n\nBetter: Use Python one-liner if python3 exists. Let's check.\n</think>\nI see the issue — the script is picking up author IDs as well as message IDs because both have `\"id\":\"...`\" in the JSON. That's why it's trying to delete user IDs (404). Let me fix the extraction to only get top-level message IDs:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_46fbe5e133b34ff2867682b6","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/root/.openclaw/workspace/delete_channel_proper.sh","content":"#!/bin/bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nCHANNEL_ID=\"1471005448948813874\"\nTOKEN=\"MTQ3MDk5MDc0MzA5ODIzMzExMg.GS1nWd.02_4znxHWOy_xbn5yVqx9UScxQQNLyrsyr80Ys\"\nBASE=\"https://discord.com/api/v10\"\n\ntotal_deleted=0\nbefore=\"\"\nbackoff=0.5\n\nwhile :; do\n  # Fetch up to 100 messages (oldest first via before parameter)\n  if [ -z \"$before\" ]; then\n    response=$(curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bot $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n      \"$BASE/channels/$CHANNEL_ID/messages?limit=100\")\n  else\n    response=$(curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bot $TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n      \"$BASE/channels/$CHANNEL_ID/messages?limit=100&before=$before\")\n  fi\n\n  # Extract only top-level message IDs (avoid author.id, etc.)\n  # We look for lines like:  \"id\":\"MESSAGE_ID\",  at the start of a message object\n  # Simplest: use sed to strip objects and keep only the first id per object\n  ids=($(echo \"$response\" | sed