already points to references like `references/workflows.md`. But currently those references don't exist. So if we create them, we would actually add files, not reduce tokens. However, the idea is that those reference files are not loaded into context unless needed (when the skill is triggered). So by moving detailed instructions out of AGENTS.md into separate reference files, we reduce the always-loaded context. But we must ensure that when the agent needs that information, it can load the reference file on demand. That requires that the agent knows to read those files when needed. AGENTS.md would have a pointer like \"For heartbeat procedures, see references/heartbeat-guide.md\". That pointer itself is short. So indeed we can reduce AGENTS.md size.\n\nBut the skill creation guide is currently in MEMORY.md. That is totally misplaced. It should be either removed (since we have a skill-creator skill) or moved to a skill. If we remove it, we save a lot.\n\nLet me verify the content of MEMORY.md more carefully from the read. It includes:\n\n```\n# Long-term Memory\n\n## Model Providers\n- NVIDIA...\n- OpenRouter...\n\n## User Preferences\n...\n\n## Configuration Notes\n...\n\n### 🧠 MEMORY.md - Your Long-Term Memory\n- **ONLY load in main session**...\n...\n\n## Technical Notes\n\n- Discord plugin is installed but was disabled; enabled via config\n...\n\n## Later Updates (05:30–05:50 UTC)\n...\n\n## Learnings\n...\n\n## Skill Creation Process\n...\n```\n\nActually there is a section \"### 🧠 MEMORY.md - Your Long-Term Memory\" which duplicates some of what AGENTS.md says about MEMORY.md. That's redundant. And then \"## Technical Notes\" about Discord. That's fine. \"## Later Updates\" is also memory. \"## Learnings\" is memory. But then \"## Skill Creation Process\" is clearly a guide. That's not memory. So that is misplaced.\n\nAlso the \"## Technical Notes\" about Discord integration might be considered memory, but it's more like a log of actions. That's okay, but maybe