that should always be known. However, we could split into a reference file that is loaded on demand when the agent is in a group chat? The agent could decide to read it when needed, but it's not invoked as a skill; it's a reference. The instruction says \"Keep local notes ... in `TOOLS.md`\". So reference files are usually placed under references/ directory and loaded when needed. But we don't have a mechanism to auto-load references; the agent must decide to read them. In practice, the agent might not read them unless triggered. But the idea is that the skill system might support loading reference files when needed. Actually the skill guidelines say that reference files are loaded as needed. So we could create a references/group-chat-guidelines.md and then in AGENTS.md we say: \"For group chat behavior guidelines, see references/group-chat-guidelines.md\". Then during operation, when the agent detects it's in a group chat, it might read that file. That would reduce always-loaded tokens.\n\nBut is that desirable? The guidelines say \"Keep local notes ... in TOOLS.md\". That suggests that such notes are not always loaded; they are read when needed. Actually, in the agent's process, it reads AGENTS.md at start, then reads USER.md, then memory files. It does not automatically read TOOLS.md at start. Looking at AGENTS.md \"Every Session\" steps: it doesn't mention reading TOOLS.md. So TOOLS.md is likely not automatically loaded. It says \"Keep local notes ... in TOOLS.md.\" So the agent might read TOOLS.md only when it needs those notes. So TOOLS.md is already designed as a reference, not always-loaded. But currently TOOLS.md is just placeholder instructions. So it's not adding much token load because it's not being read every message? Wait: The startup sequence says: \"Before doing anything else: 1. Read SOUL.md 2. Read USER.md 3. Read memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md (today + yesterday) for recent context 4. If in MAIN SESSION: Also read MEMORY.md\". It does NOT say read AGENTS.md! But AGENTS.md itself is read implicitly? Actually the system loads AGENTS.md automatically? The description says: \"Your Workspace\" suggests it's used by the agent. Indeed, the agent's initialization likely reads AGENTS.md to get these instructions. So AGENTS.md is loaded every session? Possibly. But the token count per message may not include AGENTS.md if it's only loaded at session start and then the session maintains state. Actually the memory summary mentions: \"These files (AGENTS.md, TOOLS.md, USER.md, MEMORY.md, HEARTBEAT.md, SOUL.md) are injected into the system prompt at the start of each agent turn.\" I recall from OpenClaw docs: These files are included in the system prompt for every turn? Let's check: In the initial Project Context, it says: \"The following project context files have been loaded: If SOUL.md is present, embody its persona and tone...\". That suggests they are loaded at start and maybe stay in context. But are they re-injected every message? The system prompt likely includes them persistently as part of the context window across turns. In many agents, the system prompt is fixed and reused. So they are loaded once and stay in context throughout the session. So they consume tokens all the time. So reducing them reduces context window usage.\n\nThus, we should aim to reduce the always-loaded set (SOUL, USER, AGENTS, maybe HEARTBEAT, maybe a trimmed MEMORY? But MEMORY is only for main sessions). Also maybe TOOLS is not always loaded; but the agent might read it when needed; that does not affect always-loaded tokens because it loads on demand? Actually the system prompt does not include TOOLS.md; the agent reads it explicitly when needed. The same for references. So tokens from TOOLS.md are only consumed when the agent reads it. So we don't need to worry about TOOLS.md for always-loaded savings. But the user asked \"Audit every file that loads into your context each message\". Which files load into context each message? That would be the on